  "body": "Fixes #40. Thanks to @robert-010 for the diagnosis and patch — this is essentially their fix.\n\n> Stacked on #44 → #43 → #42. Review the last commit.\n\n### The bug\n\nConfirmed exactly as reported. `get_transaction_impl` omitted `original_order` from its SELECT and filled the field via `..Default::default()`, producing `None`. `update_transaction_impl` then wrote *every* column back, binding that `None` as NULL. Fetch-incomplete, write-complete.\n\nBecause NULL sorts last under `ORDER BY original_order ASC NULLS LAST`, every updated row was written to the bottom of the Transactions tab on the next `sync up`, below the oldest row, regardless of date.\n\nThe `categories` and `autocat` getters list all their fields explicitly, so they never had the problem — matching the report that only transactions were affected.\n\n### The fix\n\nThe SELECT now includes `original_order` and the field is populated from it.\n\nOne addition beyond the reported patch: the struct literal no longer ends in `..Default::default()`. Every remaining field is listed explicitly, so a field added to `Transaction` later becomes a compile error at this call site instead of silently reading back as its default. The one field that genuinely has no column to read from is `no_name`, now set explicitly with a comment explaining why.\n\n### Verification\n\nTwo tests:\n\n- `test_update_transactions_preserves_original_order` — the regression from the issue. Verified it **fails** on the unpatched code (`assertion left == right` at the `original_order` comparison) and passes with the fix.\n- `test_update_transactions_leaves_local_rows_unordered` — a locally-inserted row has no `original_order`, and updating it must not invent one. This guards the other direction, so the fix can't be \"fixed\" by defaulting to zero.\n\n`cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy -- -D warnings`, and `cargo test` (194 tests) all pass.\n\n### One thing noticed nearby\n\n`Transaction::no_name` — the unnamed column A that some Tiller sheets have — has no column in the `transactions` table at all, so its contents are dropped on any `sync down` → `sync up` round trip. That is a separate latent issue from this one and isn't touched here; happy to open an issue for it if you'd like.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01K8P9GMZo3N7hiGrxL91f8M)_",
